// @id 01M49AZX6S5RHHZS8ETS0X24R0
q: The ==ampere (symbol: A, often shortened to amp)== is the standard unit of electric current. An electric current of one unit is one coulomb per second.
e: The amp is named after André-Marie Ampère who studied electromagnetism.

// @id 01M49AZX6SA7VNQQPQ1X04Y1H4
q: The ==coulomb (symbol: C)== is the SI unit of electric charge. It is defined to be equal to the electric charge delivered by a 1 ampere current in 1 second.
e: It is named after Charles-Augustin de Coulomb.

// @id 01M49AZX6SQPZP4W5ZK9187AS1
q: The ==volt (symbol: V)== is the SI derived unit of electric potential difference or electromotive force (also known as voltage). It is defined as the potential difference across a conductor when a current of one ampere dissipates one watt of power.
e: It is named in honor of the Italian physicist Alessandro Volta.

// @id 01M49AZX6SR1M5SWC9EM208GVK
q: The ==ohm (symbol: Ω)== is the unit of electrical resistance. It is defined as an electrical resistance between two points of a conductor when a constant potential difference of one volt (V), applied to these points, produces in the conductor a current of one ampere (A), the conductor not being the seat of any electromotive force.
e: It is named after German physicist Georg Ohm (1789–1854).

// @id 01M49AZX6S4S5W9EWF8YQZDAM9
q: The ==henry (symbol: H)== is the unit of inductance. The inductance of an electric circuit is one unit when an electric current that is changing at one ampere per second results in an electromotive force of one volt across the inductor.
e: It is named after Joseph Henry.

// @id 01M49AZX6SSM6KHD4D6QEBDHXN model=basic-example
q: What is the relation between volt, ampere, and ohm?
a: $$R = \frac{V}{I}$$
